ntial undoing","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Four deconstructive techniques common to both traditions","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The teacher-student dynamic","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Four key deconstructive techniques","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Unfindability analysis","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Bringing everything back to the here and now","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Paradoxical problems","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Negation","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Dynamics of experiential undoing","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Non-dual experiential 'space'","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Experiential mapping : practitioners in the space","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Experiential undoing in Advaita Vedānta","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Experiential undoing in Zen Buddhism","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Conclusion: deconstruction of reified awareness.","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}}],"covers":[8613880],"lc_classifications":["B132.A3 D38 2010","B132.A3D38 2010"],"key":"/books/OL24534517M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL6748251A","name":"Leesa S. Davis"}],"publish_places":["London","New York"],"isbn_13":["9780826420688"],"pagination":"xxi, 222 p. ;","source_records":["marc:marc_loc_updates/v38.i26.records.utf8:20932174:3081","marc:marc_loc_updates/v37.i43.records.utf8:42269028:2670","ia:advaitavedantaze00davi","bwb:9780826420688","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part36.utf8:147778123:3081","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-016.mrc:159269824:3599","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/ab.bib.12.20150123.full.mrc:317185936:3218","marc:marc_bloomsbury/2025MAR_bloomsbury.mrc:47949071:3643"],"title":"Advaita Vedānta and Zen Buddhism","lccn":["2009043205"],"number_of_pages":222,"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"dewey_decimal_class":["181/.482"],"isbn_10":["0826420680"],"publish_date":"2010","publish_country":"enk","by_statement":"Leesa S. Davis","oclc_numbers":["432983505","635947390"],"works":[{"key":"/works/OL15582390W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"\"This fascinating and innovative book explores the relationship between the philosophical underpinnings of Advaita Vedanta, Zen Buddhism and the experiential journey of spiritual practitioners. Taking the perspective of the questioning student, the author highlights the experiential deconstructive processes that are ignited when students' \"everyday\" dualistic thought structures are challenged by the non-dual nature of these teachings and practices. Although Advaita Vedanta and Zen Buddhism are ontologically different, this unique study shows that in the dynamics of the practice situation they are phenomenologically similar. Distinctive in scope and approach Advaita Vedanta and Zen Buddhism: Deconstructive Modes of Spiritual Inquiry examines Advaita and Zen as living practice traditions in which foundational non-dual philosophies are shown \"in action\" in contemporary Western practice situations thus linking abstract philosophical tenets to concrete living experience. As such it takes an important step toward bridging the gap between scholarly analysis and the experiential reality of these spiritual practices.\"--Bloomsbury Publishing."},"identifiers":{"doi":["10.5040/9781472548450"]},"latest_revision":10,"revision":10,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2010-12-15T03:51:01.403875"},"last_m